  "beats": [
    "rapid-in",
    "r-acid",
    "r-norm",
    "r-joins",
    "r-keys",
    "r-cover",
    "next"
  ],
  "text": "Five more, a sentence or two each. Acid: atomicity, all or nothing; consistency, every constraint holds; isolation, concurrent transactions don't corrupt each other; durability, a commit survives a crash. Normalization stores each fact once, so no update leaves copies disagreeing, which suits write-heavy systems. Denormalization repeats data to skip joins, buying read speed with storage and harder updates. Normalize first; denormalize where profiling blames a join. Joins differ in which unmatched rows survive. Inner keeps only matches: twenty-four rows. Left keeps every customer, with nulls where there's no order: thirty, for the six who never ordered. Right mirrors it, and full keeps both sides. A primary key identifies each row: unique, never null, and indexed automatically. A foreign key points at another table's primary key, so no order can name a missing customer. Postgres doesn't index the foreign key's column for you. A covering index holds every column a query needs, so Postgres answers from the index alone, with zero trips to the table, at the price of a wider index. Isolation, locks and deadlocks are the database's side of one problem: many requests arriving at once. Next: concurrency.",
